<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Usumacinta River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Usumacinta River rises in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and flows north-west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, for much of its course forming the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and Guatemala. In the Classic period it was a major trade route of the Maya world, lined by rival kingdoms such as Yaxchilan and Piedras Negras. It is the largest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> by volume.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usumacinta-river.jpg" alt="Usumacinta River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ProtoplasmaKid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%ADo_Usumacinta_a_la_altura_de_Boca_del_Cerro_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
